 ### KD-Tree Partitioned Volume Rendering
-A method to avoid computing all scalar values regardless of visibility by partitioning the volume into smaller sub-volumes with similar properties based on the transfer function [S3, unofficial]. The opacity map corresponds to a region of the frame buffer and stores the minimum opacity of the frame buffer pixels found within that region [S3, unofficial]. It groups scalar values within a certain range together based on the transfer function to define sub-volume properties [S3, unofficial]. A kd-tree is used to render the partitioned volume with correct visibility order, where each node is associated with a sub-volume [S3, unofficial]. Each sub-volume is culled and clipped against an opacity map [S3, unofficial]. Reported performance was 10 fps for the Engine data set using a 2.5 GHz Pentium-IV and a nVidia GeForce4 graphics adapter [S3, unofficial].
+A method to avoid computing all scalar values regardless of visibility by partitioning the volume into smaller sub-volumes with similar properties based on the transfer function [S3, unofficial]. The opacity map corresponds to a region of the frame buffer and stores the minimum opacity of the frame buffer pixels found within that region [S3, unofficial]. It groups scalar values within a certain range together based on the transfer function to define sub-volume properties, uses a kd-tree to render the partitioned volume with correct visibility order (where each node is associated with a sub-volume), and culls/clips each sub-volume against the opacity map [S3, unofficial]. Reported performance was 10 fps for the Engine data set using a 2.5 GHz Pentium-IV and a nVidia GeForce4 graphics adapter [S3, unofficial].
 
 ### Mipmap Table Construction
-Constructs a lookup table for hierarchical grid traversal. It maps coarse grid voxels to their fine grid children [S1, unofficial]. It identifies occupied voxels in the fine grid and assigns a unique index to each [S1, unofficial]. It extracts the 8 sub-grid regions corresponding to the 8 children of a coarse voxel by striding the fine grid indices [S1, unofficial]. It flattens these sub-grids and filters them using the coarse grid mask to select only the children that belong to occupied coarse voxels [S1, unofficial]. It initializes the table with shape `[num_coarse_voxels, 11]` (8 child indices + 3 coordinates), filled with -1 [S1, unofficial]. It populates columns 0-7 with the filtered child indices and columns 8-10 with the coarse voxel coordinates [S1, unofficial].
+Constructs a lookup table for hierarchical grid traversal. It maps coarse grid voxels to their fine grid children. The table is built from a fine grid and a coarse grid mask [S1, unofficial]. Steps include: identifying occupied voxels in the fine grid and assigning unique indices; extracting the 8 sub-grid regions corresponding to the 8 children of a coarse voxel by striding the fine grid indices; flattening these sub-grids and filtering them using the coarse grid mask to select only children belonging to occupied coarse voxals; initializing the table with shape `[num_coarse_voxels, 11]` (8 child indices + 3 coordinates) filled with -1; populating columns 0-7 with the filtered child indices; and populating columns 8-10 with the coarse voxel coordinates [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The traversal time depends on the sparsity of the volume and the depth of the hierarchy. In the worst case (fully dense volume), it visits all leaf voxels, similar to flat DDA, but with the overhead of table lookups. In sparse volumes, it skips large empty regions, reducing the number of visited nodes significantly. The cost per level is proportional to the number of intersected voxels at that level.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the mipmap levels and the lookup tables. The lookup table size is proportional to the number of non-empty coarse voxels at each level. For a base resolution of $N$, the table size is manageable if the volume is sparse.
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient for sparse volumes; reduces memory bandwidth by skipping empty space; simple implementation using lookup tables; compatible with GPU vectorized execution (as seen in `ray_mipmap_intersect_vectorized`) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-computation of the mipmap and lookup tables; less flexible for dynamic data compared to tree-based structures like Octrees; precision issues can arise if epsilon parameters are not tuned correctly [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Sparse volumetric data where empty space skipping is critical; static volumes where pre-computation is feasible.
-*   **When not to use:** Dense volumes where the overhead of table lookups outweighs the benefits; highly dynamic data where rebuilding the hierarchy is too expensive.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The traversal time depends on the sparsity of the volume and the depth of the hierarchy. In the best case (large empty regions), the ray skips entire branches of the hierarchy, resulting in $O(\log N)$ steps per ray, where $N$ is the number of finest-level voxels. In the worst case (fully dense volume), it degrades to $O(N)$ but with higher overhead due to table lookups compared to flat DDA.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires $O(N_{coarse})$ memory for the lookup tables, where $N_{coarse}$ is the number of voxels in the coarsest levels. The tables are typically small and fit in cache, improving performance over pointer-based trees [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Specific benchmarks for the base Hierarchical Grid Traversal method are not provided in the sources. However, related methods like KD-Tree Partitioned Volume Rendering reported 10 fps for the Engine data set on a 2.5 GHz Pentium-IV and nVidia GeForce4 [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient skipping of empty space; cache-friendly data layout (lookup tables); exact intersection results (no approximation errors from skipping).
+*   **Limitations:** Requires precomputation of the mipmap tables; less flexible than dynamic octrees for changing data; potential for precision issues with small epsilon values [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Sparse volumetric data with large empty regions; static datasets where precomputation is feasible.
+*   **When Not to Use:** Highly dense volumes where the overhead of table lookups outweighs the benefits of skipping; dynamic data that changes frequently.
 
 ## Background
-Hierarchical Grid Traversal, also known as Ray-Mipmap Traversal, is a technique used in volume rendering to accelerate ray marching through multi-resolution grids. It is closely related to the concept of mipmapping in texture mapping, where lower-resolution versions of the data are used to quickly determine if a ray intersects a region of interest. The method is implemented in various open-source projects, such as the Jax-Volumetric-Renderer and HNanoSolver, which demonstrate its application in modern, differentiable, and high-performance rendering pipelines [S1, S2, unofficial]. The approach is particularly relevant for sparse volume data, where traditional DDA traversal would waste significant time traversing empty space.
+Hierarchical Grid Traversal, also known as Ray-Mipmap Traversal, is a technique for accelerating volume rendering by utilizing multi-resolution grid structures. It builds on the principles of ray-grid traversal [17-ray-grid-traversal.md] and hierarchical data structures [4392-hierarchical-and-block-based-data-structures.md]. The method is implemented in open-source projects such as [Jax-Volumetric-Renderer](https://github.com/szat/Jax-Volumetric-Renderer) [S1, unofficial] and [HNanoSolver](https://github.com/ZephirFXEC/HNanoSolver) [S2, unofficial]. The concept of partitioning volumes and using hierarchical structures for visibility culling has been explored in academic literature, such as the work by Li et al. on KD-Tree partitioned volume rendering [S3, unofficial].
 
